WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:05.640
You’ve almost certainly heard of MrBeast
— the 15th-largest YouTube channel by subscriber

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.030
count, made famous for doing extreme stunts.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:14.559
When you see a MrBeast thumbnail, your first
thought is “There’s no way that’s real”.

00:00:14.559 --> 00:00:19.390
There’s no way he actually gave a random
fan 40 cars.

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:23.730
There’s no way he actually bought a private
island,

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.820
Or planted 20 million trees.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:31.330
If the internet has taught us anything, it’s:
don’t believe what you see.

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:35.850
Today, if you really were the one-millionth
visitor, you’d never know.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:39.910
And yet, Jimmy Donaldson actually follows
through.

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:46.390
It’s honest clickbait, in a way that brings
back some of that early internet magic.

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:49.630
And in December, MrBeast did it again.

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:54.309
This time, by opening “MrBeast Burger”
— an actual restaurant where he handed out

00:00:54.309 --> 00:00:59.739
free stacks of cash, Apple products, and,
yes, even a few burgers.

00:00:59.739 --> 00:01:04.629
At the end of the video, viewers at home were
told they too could order a burger from one

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:08.680
of its 300 locations around the country.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:15.810
Residents of towns as small as Evanston, Wyoming,
Population: 11,000, and Topsham, Maine, Population:

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:21.720
8,000, were surprised to find they too had
a local MrBeast Burger location.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.800
The app was so popular, in fact, that it crashed
on that first day.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:30.119
2 months later, over a million burgers had
been sold.

00:01:30.119 --> 00:01:35.920
Now, MrBeast had the unique advantage of not
needing to spend a dime on marketing, which

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:41.950
might make this whole concept look like just
another influencer brand or publicity stunt.

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:47.030
But the most fascinating thing about MrBeast
Burger is the fact that it’s actually not

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:48.200
unique.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:54.220
The way he opened 300 locations in only a
matter of months is the same way tens of thousands

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:59.110
of restaurants have stayed alive during the
pandemic, and a sign of where the restaurant

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:01.729
industry might be headed.

00:02:01.729 --> 00:02:07.340
Sponsored by CuriosityStream and Nebula — where
you can watch the extended version of this

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:08.340
video.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:12.480
No industry has been left untouched by the
pandemic.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.010
But some have been forced to adapt more than
others.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:20.450
While airlines were handed over $25 billion
in direct grants,

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:24.310
Small, local restaurants were left to fend
for themselves.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:30.650
According to the National Restaurant Association,
$131 billion in sales were lost in just the

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:33.930
first five months of the pandemic alone.

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:39.700
Tragic as these circumstances were, they unintentionally
created the optimal conditions for a kind

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.080
of restaurant revolution.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:47.570
Just as residents of New York City, LA, and
the Bay Area found themselves paying insane

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:53.540
rent with no access to the city they loved
and decided to take their money elsewhere,

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:56.420
restaurant owners soon came to that same realization.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:03:02.010
They were still paying for the best real estate,
but, who, on the other side of a delivery

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:05.350
app, would notice if they moved across town?

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:06.950
And why stop there?

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:13.400
If customers never see inside the restaurant,
why pay for decorations, bathrooms, even signs

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:14.520
or tables?

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:19.330
That’s the basic premise behind what are
called “Cloud”, “Virtual”, “Dark”,

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:25.470
or “Ghost” Kitchens — somewhat ambiguous
terms for restaurants that don’t exist — at

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:28.610
least, not in the way we typically think of
them.

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:32.780
If you’ve ordered food delivery in the past
few years, and if the name of the restaurant

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:38.349
sounded a bit strange or unfamiliar, there’s
a decent chance you’ve already ordered from

00:03:38.349 --> 00:03:39.349
one.

00:03:39.349 --> 00:03:42.650
That’s because they aren’t entirely new.

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:46.640
Restaurants and food trucks have long been
able to rent “Commissary kitchens”, as

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.670
they’re known in the industry, to prepare
their food.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:54.820
In recent times, however, and especially as
a response to the pandemic, restaurants have

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:58.820
found clever ways of taking this concept further.

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:03.630
One company, called “Reef Technology”,
for example, owns thousands of parking lots

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:07.990
across the country, which, of course, have
mostly sat empty.

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:13.900
To make up for lost revenue, it placed delivery-only
kitchen trailers in over 90 of its parking

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:17.979
lots, with recipes licensed from other restaurants.

00:04:17.979 --> 00:04:23.639
Kroger, the country’s largest grocery chain,
did something similar by launching ghost kitchens

00:04:23.639 --> 00:04:25.819
inside several of its stores.

00:04:25.819 --> 00:04:34.089
Red Robin, Applebee’s, Chipotle, and Wendy’s,
to name just a few, also now operate delivery-only

00:04:34.089 --> 00:04:35.249
locations.

00:04:35.249 --> 00:04:41.430
Other restaurants stayed put but found new
ways to extract value from their fixed assets:

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:46.539
One of the biggest problems with restaurants,
in general, is that, for the most part, people

00:04:46.539 --> 00:04:51.719
only eat during certain times of the day,
which means equipment, staff, and valuable

00:04:51.719 --> 00:04:56.520
real estate sit unused for most of the 24-hour
day.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.849
But nothing says they have to.

00:04:58.849 --> 00:05:04.349
In the same way that AirBnB and Turo allow
individuals to make extra money from the time

00:05:04.349 --> 00:05:09.569
they’re not using their home or car, many
restaurants now rent out their unused kitchen

00:05:09.569 --> 00:05:14.289
time — which is how MrBeast pulled off MrBeast
Burger.

00:05:14.289 --> 00:05:17.839
Your delivery driver will never see a MrBeast
sign.

00:05:17.839 --> 00:05:20.789
Instead, they might be directed to a Bravo!

00:05:20.789 --> 00:05:26.949
Italian Kitchen or Heroes Grill — real restaurants
whose kitchens have been hired by MrBeast

00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:30.520
to produce his burgers using his recipes.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:37.529
Guy Fieri also partnered with existing restaurants
to create 130 “Flavortown Kitchens”.

00:05:37.529 --> 00:05:43.199
Another way for restaurants to make some extra
cash is by launching their own ghost kitchens.

00:05:43.199 --> 00:05:49.139
A Mexican restaurant, for example, might launch
a delivery-only brand of Japanese food operating

00:05:49.139 --> 00:05:53.979
out of the same kitchen, without any of its
customers knowing the difference.

00:05:53.979 --> 00:05:57.860
You can even buy a ghost kitchen franchise
package.

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:03.509
For an up-front fee of $2,000, Chicago-based
“Wow Bao” will send you all the training

00:06:03.509 --> 00:06:08.009
and equipment you need to sell its Asian food
out of your restaurant.

00:06:08.009 --> 00:06:10.869
In fact, some have turned this into a feature.

00:06:10.869 --> 00:06:15.240
Since multiple brands are cooked from the
same kitchen, they allow you to mix-and-match

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:17.990
cuisines for the same delivery fee.

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:24.509
So, for example, you could order some Tacos,
Sushi, and Bubble Tea all at the same time.

00:06:24.509 --> 00:06:29.389
Usually, though, customers have no idea where
their food comes from.

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:34.180
Last year, for instance, a woman in Philadelphia
ordered pizza from what she believed to be

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:36.169
a locally-owned restaurant.

00:06:36.169 --> 00:06:41.100
When it arrived, it tasted a bit familiar,
and not in a good way.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:45.630
After doing some digging, she discovered this
“local” restaurant was actually Chuck

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:48.610
E. Cheese, using a fictional name on Grubhub.

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:54.999
It’s reminiscent of the white-label industry
on Amazon — where sellers buy, repackage,

00:06:54.999 --> 00:06:58.159
and resell products under different brand
names.

00:06:58.159 --> 00:07:05.029
For better or worse, this is the next frontier
in the industry — the Amazon-ification of

00:07:05.029 --> 00:07:06.490
food.

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:09.360
Kitchens are slowly becoming anonymous warehouses.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:15.370
You don’t know or care where your packages
come from, and increasingly neither do we

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:17.750
know or care where our food does.

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:23.860
At least, that’s the hope of companies like
CloudKitchens — the WeWork for restaurants,

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:29.399
funded with a $400 million investment by Saudi
Arabia’s sovereign-wealth fund.

00:07:29.399 --> 00:07:34.559
The company starts by shopping around for
cheap, distressed properties — dying bowling

00:07:34.559 --> 00:07:38.139
alleys, auto repair shops, and warehouses.

00:07:38.139 --> 00:07:43.669
Within each of these buildings, it constructs
tiny 200 square foot kitchen “cubicles”

00:07:43.669 --> 00:07:46.969
— which chefs can rent on a monthly basis.

00:07:46.969 --> 00:07:53.860
These shared spaces contain everything a delivery-only
restaurant needs — grills, sinks, WiFi,

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:57.020
electricity, dishwashers, and so on.

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:01.800
Some might argue this takes away all the fun
of owning and operating a restaurant, but

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:04.169
there are also advantages.

00:08:04.169 --> 00:08:08.219
Think about it this way:
There are good chefs and there are good business

00:08:08.219 --> 00:08:09.219
people.

00:08:09.219 --> 00:08:11.330
Some people are both, but not all.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:16.249
That means, right now we’re missing out
on a lot of good food simply because some

00:08:16.249 --> 00:08:19.319
good chefs don’t have good business skills.

00:08:19.319 --> 00:08:25.080
Services like CloudKitchens lower the barrier
to entry, which moves the two circles closer

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:26.220
together.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:31.520
The parallels between CloudKitchens and Silicon
Valley are no coincidence.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:37.360
The man behind CloudKitchens is none other
than Travis Kalanick — former CEO of Uber,

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:41.919
before he was forced to “resign” following
a long string of scandals.

00:08:41.919 --> 00:08:44.810
The question is: Why restaurants?

00:08:44.810 --> 00:08:51.089
For all his tech-bro antics and reprehensible
behavior, no-one doubts Kalanick’s strategic

00:08:51.089 --> 00:08:52.540
business mind.

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:59.120
And yet, the restaurant industry is famously
low margin and high risk.

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:04.440
Food is not software — there are very few
overnight successes, and there are natural,

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:06.149
physical limits on growth.

00:09:06.149 --> 00:09:11.019
There’s a reason, in other words, none of
the world’s largest companies are involved

00:09:11.019 --> 00:09:12.160
in food.

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:19.230
And why is loud, flashy, outspoken Kalanick,
hiding from the spotlight, making sure CloudKitchens

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:21.360
keeps a low media profile?

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:28.120
It would seem Kalanick is quietly biding his
time, waiting to launch a revolution of the

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:29.920
restaurant industry.

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:35.950
CloudKitchens wants to become the Amazon of
food — and, if anyone could pull it off,

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:39.089
it’s probably the man behind Uber.

00:09:39.089 --> 00:09:43.839
This is where I explain Kalanick’s vision
for the future of food, and why he might just

00:09:43.839 --> 00:09:48.800
be on to something, in the extended version
of this video on Nebula.

